{"name":"Pale Brown","hex":"#b1916e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pale","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Light in color.","Having a pallor (a light color, especially due to sickness, shock, fright etc.)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To turn pale; to lose colour.","To become insignificant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pale"},{"term":"brown","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour like that of chocolate or coffee.","One of the colour balls used in snooker, with a value of 4 points."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a brown color/colour.","Gloomy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brown"}],"entries":[{"title":"Shades of brown","blocks":[{"text":"Shades of brown can be produced by combining red, yellow, and black pigments, or by a combination of orange and black—illustrated in the color box. The RGB color model, that generates all colors on computer and television screens, makes brown by combining red and green light at relatively low intensities, with green light being in a lesser proportion than red light. Brown color names are often imprecise, and some shades, such as beige, can refer to lighter rather than darker shades of yellow and red. Such colors are less saturated than colors perceived to be orange. Browns are usually described as light or dark, reddish, yellowish, or gray-brown. There are no standardized names for shades of brown; the same shade may have different names on different color lists, and sometimes one name can refer to several very different colors. The X11 color list of web colors has seventeen different shades of brown, but the complete list of browns is much longer."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Color_icon_brown_v2.svg/330px-Color_icon_brown_v2.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_brown","wikidata":"Q17079449"},{"title":"Brown","blocks":[{"text":"Brown is a color. It can be thought as a darker, typically desaturated shade of orange, and can often be produced by combining red and yellow with another color, namely blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg/330px-Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown","wikidata":"Q47071"},{"title":"Colorfulness","blocks":[{"text":"Colorfulness, chroma and saturation are attributes of perceived color relating to chromatic intensity. As defined formally by the International Commission on Illumination (CIE) they respectively describe three different aspects of chromatic intensity, but the terms are often used loosely and interchangeably in contexts where these aspects are not clearly distinguished. The precise meanings of the terms vary by what other functions they are dependent on."},{"items":["Colorfulness is the \"attribute of a visual perception according to which the perceived color of an area appears to be more or less chromatic \". The colorfulness evoked by an object depends not only on its spectral reflectance but also on the strength of the illumination, and increases with the latter unless the brightness is very high.","Chroma is the \"colorfulness of an area judged as a proportion of the brightness of a similarly illuminated area that appears white or highly transmitting\". As a result, chroma is mostly only dependent on the spectral properties, and as such is seen to describe the object color. It is how different from a grey of the same lightness such an object color appears to be.","Saturation is the \"colorfulness of an area judged in proportion to its brightness\", which in effect is the perceived freedom from whitishness of the light coming from the area. An object with a given spectral reflectance exhibits approximately constant saturation for all levels of illumination, unless the brightness is very high."]}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Brightness_and_colorfulness.png/330px-Brightness_and_colorfulness.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colorfulness","wikidata":"Q27673"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/American_Beaver.jpg/500px-American_Beaver.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/American_Beaver.jpg/500px-American_Beaver.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAmerican_Beaver.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Royal_Winter_Fair_Wool_2.jpg/500px-Royal_Winter_Fair_Wool_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Royal_Winter_Fair_Wool_2.jpg/1280px-Royal_Winter_Fair_Wool_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Royal_Winter_Fair_Wool_2.jpg/500px-Royal_Winter_Fair_Wool_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARoyal_Winter_Fair_Wool_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Chamois-natural.jpg/500px-Chamois-natural.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Chamois-natural.jpg/1280px-Chamois-natural.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Chamois-natural.jpg/500px-Chamois-natural.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=pars